course: dictionary
lesson: 5320
title: Kennings: clever names in poems
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A kenning is a short, two-word phrase that names something in a clever, poetic way instead of using its usual name. The sea can be called a «whale-road», and the sun can be called a «sky-candle». Each phrase paints a picture, so you have to think about what the words mean together.
explain: Kennings are often used in poems. A kenning poem is a list of kennings that describe one thing, and the reader guesses what it is. Here is one for a cat: «mouse-catcher», «lap-warmer», «sofa-scratcher». Can you guess the cat from its clues?
explain: The example «ball-kicker» means a footballer, because a footballer kicks a ball. Kennings do not need to rhyme. You only need two words that name something in a new way.
fact: A kenning is a short, two-word phrase.
fact: A kenning names something in a clever, poetic way instead of using its usual name.
fact: «Whale-road» is a kenning for the sea, and «sky-candle» is a kenning for the sun.
fact: Kennings were used a lot in Old English and Old Norse poems, such as «Beowulf» and Viking sagas.
fact: Kennings do not need to rhyme.
exercise: In the kenning «sky-candle», what is being described? | The moon | A lamp | A star | *The sun
exercise: Which phrase is a kenning for the sea? | Green-field | Fish-bowl | *Whale-road | Blue-sky
exercise: Kennings were used a lot in which kind of writing? | *Old English and Old Norse poems | Pop song lyrics | Cookery books | Modern text messages
test: Why is «mouse-catcher» a kenning for a cat? | *It names the cat in a clever way, by what it does | It is a long word | It rhymes with cat | It is the cat's usual name
test: Do kennings need to rhyme? | *No, kennings do not need to rhyme | Yes, every line must rhyme | Only if they are in «Beowulf» | Only in songs
exam: Which two-word phrase is a kenning for the sun? | *Sky-candle | Round-ball | Bright-yellow | Warm-day
